WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.880
Who went on to work with animals after a career
on the Disney Channel? Does the kid from Eerie,

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:11.700
Indiana have an appropriately weird job now
that he's an adult? Not all former child stars

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.740
burn out, some of them just quietly move on
to other professions.

00:00:16.177 --> 00:00:22.280
In Stanley Kubrick's classic 1980 adaptation
of Stephen King's The Shining, the role of

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:27.870
the troubled, psychically-gifted Danny Torrance
was played by newcomer Danny Lloyd. Lloyd

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:32.411
discussed his time making the horror film
in a 2017 interview with The Guardian, as

00:00:32.411 --> 00:00:36.750
he revealed that he enjoyed the experience
and has fond memories of it. But he hasn't

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:41.220
kept up an acting career in the decades since.
His reasons for leaving the industry have

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:45.750
nothing to do with the film itself. Instead,
the work just dried up for him.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:50.300
Lloyd continued to audition, a process that
can be grueling, but he didn't mind it too

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.100
much. As he told The Guardian,

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:57.880
"I always enjoyed it. It was exciting. But
as I got a little bit older, it got kind of

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.810
boring. Then I had to tell my parents that
I was ready to quit. Which they were fine

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:06.030
with. They were never stage parents. They
made sure I had a normal upbringing."

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:10.729
These days, Lloyd is a biology professor at
a community college in Kentucky, and he's

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:13.820
happy with his showbiz legacy. According to
him,

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:18.250
"I don't regret trying acting. When I decided
to stop, I don't regret that either."

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:24.790
If you know Mara Wilson, you most likely recognize
her from early 90s family classics like Mrs.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:30.520
Doubtfire, Miracle on 34th Street, and of
course, as the title character in Matilda.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:35.390
She was one of those child stars who seemed
to be wise beyond her years. At no point did

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:40.729
it ever appear like she was mugging for the
camera or attempting too hard to be a cute

00:01:40.729 --> 00:01:45.780
kid. Instead, there was a natural quality
in her performances that was absolutely unique

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:50.659
to her, and that may very well be why her
work in those films is still fondly remembered

00:01:50.659 --> 00:01:52.290
all these years later.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:58.350
While talking to NPR in 2016, Wilson explained
that her departure from acting happened gradually,

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:02.950
almost without her even realizing it. After
her mother's passing, acting became the only

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:08.290
constant in her life, but she didn't have
the same enthusiasm for it. As puberty struck,

00:02:08.290 --> 00:02:12.620
she was embarrassed by the natural processes
of growing up, and it was difficult to go

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:18.040
through publicly. Eventually, she found that
she could survive without acting, so she instead

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:23.660
focused on writing. She composed a play called
Sheeple in 2013, and her memoir Where Am I

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.550
Now was released in 2016.

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:28.020
"Where is she now? Where did she disappear
to?"

00:02:28.645 --> 00:02:34.239
Kay Panabaker began her career in 2001 in
a short film called Temptation, and she also

00:02:34.239 --> 00:02:39.450
recorded some voices in the animated Pixar
classic Monsters Inc. Her star then rose so

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:44.550
quickly that she worked almost nonstop for
the next ten years. She showed up on TV shows

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:49.760
like Angel and Law &amp; Order: Special Victims
Unit, while also co-starring in the Disney

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:55.490
Channel series Phil of the Future. Additionally,
she appeared in TV movies like Read It and

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:59.780
Weep, alongside her older sister Danielle.
And one of the biggest titles on her resume

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:02.440
is the 2009 remake of Fame.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:08.600
Despite her continued success, Panabaker ultimately
decided to walk away from acting. She revealed

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:13.920
to her fans on Twitter her very simple reason
for leaving the entertainment industry behind,

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.410
while also sharing some life advice, as she
wrote,

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:21.550
"I just lost the love for acting. Life is
short, we spend too much time at work, gotta

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:24.129
do what you love :) and I love my job!!"

00:03:24.129 --> 00:03:28.740
That job that she loves so much is working
with animals, as she now has a gig as a zookeeper

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.540
at Disney World's Animal Kingdom.

00:03:31.483 --> 00:03:36.050
It would be hard to imagine anyone living
on Planet Earth for the past few decades who

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:41.250
hasn't heard of the Olsen Twins. The pair
absolutely dominated family entertainment

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:46.590
during the early 1990s. They got their start
playing youngest sister Michelle Tanner on

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:52.170
the iconic family sitcom Full House, and they
then shot into the pop culture stratosphere

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:57.300
thanks to their numerous TV movies like To
Grandmother's House We Go and Double, Double,

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:02.100
Toil and Trouble, as well as a mountain of
direct-to-video movies and other official

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:03.590
merchandise.

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:08.989
In 2006, the Olsen twins launched The Row,
a luxury fashion line that they've worked

00:04:08.989 --> 00:04:14.610
on perfecting for over fifteen years. In a
rare interview, they sat down with i-D Magazine

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:19.729
in 2021 to celebrate the success of their
venture. The conversation included very little

00:04:19.729 --> 00:04:24.039
discussion of their acting careers, though
they did mention that the reason they're not

00:04:24.039 --> 00:04:28.909
open with the public about their lives is
they've always been "discreet people," as

00:04:28.909 --> 00:04:33.490
that's how they were raised. While we may
not know their exact reasons for entering

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:37.699
the fashion world and leaving entertainment
behind, it seems pretty clear that they're

00:04:37.699 --> 00:04:39.407
happy with their current careers.

00:04:40.125 --> 00:04:45.889
Leanna Creel's biggest claim to fame is probably
the 10 episodes of Saved by the Bell she appeared

00:04:45.889 --> 00:04:50.539
in during the show's final season in which
she was set up as a foil for Zack Morris.

00:04:50.539 --> 00:04:56.080
She played the headstrong Tori Scott, who
wouldn't fall easily for his charm. Before

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:02.419
that, her first credit was in another beloved
series: a 1987 episode of the ABC sitcom Growing

00:05:02.419 --> 00:05:08.289
Pains. Shortly afterwards, she and her sisters
starred in the TV movies Parent Trap III and

00:05:08.289 --> 00:05:13.330
Parent Trap: Hawaiian Honeymoon, alongside
acting legend Hayley Mills, who starred in

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:17.930
the original Parent Trap movie as well as
Good Morning, Miss Bliss, the series that

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:19.930
became Saved by the Bell.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:24.330
Creel may not be acting anymore, but she's
still involved with producing visual content

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:29.199
through Creel Studio, a production company
that she runs with her wife. It's the company's

00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:33.559
commitment to philanthropy that really helps
it stand out. According to its website,

00:05:33.559 --> 00:05:39.139
"We believe The Good Life is about more than
just material comforts, it's also about living

00:05:39.139 --> 00:05:44.199
a life marked by doing good. In illuminating
all aspects of The Good Life we convey an

00:05:44.199 --> 00:05:47.569
enlightened sense of luxury lifestyle content."

00:05:47.569 --> 00:05:51.902
Creel also started an app that assists real
estate professionals in creating content.

00:05:52.658 --> 00:05:58.580
Originally a dancer, Ami Foster is probably
best remembered for two projects: the 1989

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:03.679
Shelley Long comedy Troop Beverly Hills, which
was about a troop of rich, privileged Girl

00:06:03.679 --> 00:06:09.330
Scouts, and the NBC sitcom Punky Brewster,
on which she played the rich, entitled, and

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:13.689
slightly oblivious Margaux. But those are
only two credits on a long and impressive

00:06:13.689 --> 00:06:18.189
resume that Foster assembled over the course
of an 11-year acting career.

00:06:18.189 --> 00:06:23.870
While catching up with her Punky costar Cherie
Johnson on the Cherie's World Podcast in 2021,

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:28.839
Foster discussed what she's been up to since
walking away from acting. For a brief time,

00:06:28.839 --> 00:06:32.729
she entered the music industry and moved to
Nashville. However, she wanted to be with

00:06:32.729 --> 00:06:37.689
her family in Los Angeles, and her husband
got a job there working as a firefighter.

00:06:37.689 --> 00:06:41.659
After the birth of her daughter, she decided
that she would rather be home instead of working

00:06:41.659 --> 00:06:46.627
in showbiz. So she might have the most grown-up
gig of anybody on this list: full-time mom!

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:52.380
Michael Oliver's career may have only lasted
six years, but when your first role out of

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:57.069
the gate is the meanest little kid in cinema,
there isn't much else you need to do after

00:06:57.069 --> 00:07:02.560
that. He also had small roles in other notable
movies like Forrest Gump and Eraser, but it's

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:08.409
his portrayal of Junior in Problem Child and
Problem Child 2 that left the biggest impact.

00:07:08.409 --> 00:07:13.889
In 2015, Oliver was a guest on his Problem
Child co-star Gilbert Gottfried's Amazing

00:07:13.889 --> 00:07:18.749
Colossal Podcast. The two reminisced about
working on the original film, learning from

00:07:18.749 --> 00:07:23.459
the late John Ritter, and what happened to
Oliver in the years since. Around the time

00:07:23.459 --> 00:07:28.259
that Oliver's parents pursued legal action
against the studio for failing to pay them

00:07:28.259 --> 00:07:33.369
the money they were owed, they lost their
house and he was losing interest in acting.

00:07:33.369 --> 00:07:37.979
He likened his life experience to the opposite
of a kid wondering what it must be like to

00:07:37.979 --> 00:07:42.979
be famous. To him, he was looking at the lives
of normal kids and wondering what that was

00:07:42.979 --> 00:07:46.800
like. At the time of the interview, he was
working in the tech support industry.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:53.370
The biggest title on Omri Katz's resume is
probably the primetime soap opera Dallas,

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:58.580
on which he played John Ross Ewing III. But
anyone who grew up in the early 90s will surely

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:04.709
know him best for the quirky TV show Eerie,
Indiana and the Halloween classic Hocus Pocus.

00:08:04.709 --> 00:08:10.469
He also worked with director Joe Dante in
the 1993 movie Matinee, a cult classic ode

00:08:10.469 --> 00:08:14.039
to 60s B movie schlock and cold war tensions.

00:08:14.039 --> 00:08:18.789
In March 2020, Katz posted a message on his
Instagram that he was getting into the cannabis

00:08:18.789 --> 00:08:20.770
apparel business, as he wrote,

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:25.970
"Being a member of the cannabis community
for almost 20 years now and jumping thru [sic]

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:31.330
hoops of fire to get here I'm proud to announce
our online apparel store will be opening up

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:32.330
soon."

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:45.859
The store in question is called The Mary Danksters,
and the about section on their website declares: "We, The Mary Danksters are ambassadors of the cannabis culture, with deep roots within the traditional cannabis community.

00:08:45.859 --> 00:08:48.922
We represent positivity, herb, and unity. 

00:08:49.678 --> 00:08:54.500
Ariana Richards appeared in a few films and
TV shows in the 90s that are still a big deal

00:08:54.500 --> 00:09:00.279
today. She's surely best remembered as Lex
in the original Jurassic Park. Additionally,

00:09:00.279 --> 00:09:05.560
she also appeared alongside Kevin Bacon in
1990's Tremors, she played the title character's

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:10.860
love interest in 1995's Angus, and she turned
in a heartbreaking performance as a young

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:15.170
girl being abused by her father in an episode
of Boy Meets World.

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:20.680
In 2020, Richards spoke with Amblin Entertainment
about her experience making Jurassic Park

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:25.920
and what her life's like now. A painter from
an early age, she took part in a mentorship

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:30.660
through the California Arts Club after college.
She credits everything she learned during

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:34.600
those four years with sending her down her
current path as a professional painter.

00:09:35.372 --> 00:09:40.870
Eva Amurri started her acting career in the
1992 film Bob Roberts, alongside the father

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:46.620
of her half-siblings, Tim Robbins. Her mother
is actress Susan Sarandon, and the two worked

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:52.649
together in Dead Man Walking, Earthly Possessions,
Anywhere But Here, and The Banger Sisters.

00:09:52.649 --> 00:09:58.079
Amurri also appeared on huge TV shows like
Friends and How I Met Your Mother. Her acting

00:09:58.079 --> 00:10:02.260
career was still going strong for several
years until she ultimately decided to step

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:04.129
away in 2016.

00:10:04.129 --> 00:10:08.129
Following the birth of her daughter, Amurri
realized that she couldn't raise a child to

00:10:08.129 --> 00:10:13.129
spend her life doing something that made her
happy when she wasn't doing that herself.

00:10:13.129 --> 00:10:18.600
While talking with Yahoo! Finance in 2019,
she explained that she made a list of things

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:22.699
that did make her happy. Her then-husband
told her it looked like a lifestyle blog,

00:10:22.699 --> 00:10:27.810
and he encouraged her to learn how to make
a living in an online space. Her blog "Happily

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:34.089
Eva After" then launched in 2015 and it covers
her favorite topics, which are "Style, Decor,

00:10:34.089 --> 00:10:35.440
Food, and Crafting."

00:10:36.121 --> 00:10:41.210
Even if you've never seen a single film starring
this tiny megastar, chances are you still

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:47.040
recognize the name. Simply put, there would
be no Olsen Twin empire without Shirley Temple.

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:51.860
Her image was so recognizable and popular
that it was plastered all over various kinds

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:57.350
of merchandise in the 1930s. She was such
a huge star so early in life, but then she

00:10:57.350 --> 00:11:03.200
retired from acting at the tender age of 22.
Considering that she started her life on screen

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:09.630
at the age of 3 and starred in 34 films over
a 14-year period, it's safe to say that she

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:13.569
had a full career before most people even
got started.

00:11:13.569 --> 00:11:18.660
During the late 50s and into the early 60s,
Temple briefly returned to entertainment both

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:24.010
in front of and behind the camera. She then
transitioned to a new role in 1969 when she

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:28.470
became a diplomat. As she traveled around
the world, she witnessed atrocities that put

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:35.459
her on track to becoming the ambassador to
Ghana in 1974. And then in 1989 she was appointed

00:11:35.459 --> 00:11:42.089
by President George H. W. Bush to be the first
female ambassador to Czechoslovakia.

00:11:42.089 --> 00:11:46.529
Check out one of our newest videos right here!
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite

00:11:46.529 --> 00:11:50.920
actors are coming soon. Subscribe to our YouTube
channel and hit the bell so you don't miss

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:51.510
a single one.

